WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 02:48.600
Synthetic narration · PlainSign · Mohamed Adib, solo entrant

00:00.000 --> 00:02.860
PlainSign explains wallet permissions before you sign them.

00:02.860 --> 00:07.860
A button labelled Free Mint can request permission to move an entire NFT collection.

00:07.860 --> 00:12.500
This presentation uses synthetic narration and shows the project entered by Mohamed Adib.

00:13.150 --> 00:18.570
The problem is the gap between a website's promise and a wallet request's actual meaning.

00:18.570 --> 00:21.100
Transaction data can be difficult to read.

00:21.100 --> 00:23.990
Gasless signatures can also authorize asset movement later.

00:23.990 --> 00:26.520
This wallet image is a representative illustration.

00:27.170 --> 00:30.650
PlainSign pauses supported requests before forwarding them to the wallet.

00:30.650 --> 00:35.870
It decodes the permission and shows a Safe, Caution or Danger verdict with explicit reasons.

00:35.870 --> 00:40.040
Reject stops the request using standard wallet error four thousand and one.

00:40.690 --> 00:44.690
The separate product demo exercises the extension with an automated test wallet.

00:44.690 --> 00:48.700
A collection approval and a long lived Permit Two signature trigger Danger.

00:48.700 --> 00:53.370
A normal sign in and a known wrapped Ether request illustrate lower risk patterns.

00:53.370 --> 00:54.700
No real assets move.

00:55.350 --> 00:58.730
The extension wraps supported injected Ethereum providers.

00:58.730 --> 01:02.100
Vee em decodes transactions and typed data.

01:02.100 --> 01:04.510
Public chain services enrich addresses.

01:04.510 --> 01:07.400
Open JSON rules determine the verdict.

01:07.400 --> 01:10.300
Continue forwards the original request unchanged.

01:10.300 --> 01:12.710
Optional simulation supports Sepolia only.

01:13.360 --> 01:18.970
The product focuses on the consequence of a request, including signatures that can be used later.

01:18.970 --> 01:21.080
Every reason comes from inspectable rules.

01:21.080 --> 01:27.400
Optional artificial intelligence can reword text, but cannot decide the verdict and is disabled in the public package.

01:28.050 --> 01:31.330
The goal is a clearer decision before wallet approval.

01:31.330 --> 01:33.880
There is no backend account or telemetry.

01:33.880 --> 01:38.260
These are heuristics, and a green verdict is not a safety guarantee.

01:38.260 --> 01:40.080
Provider interception is best effort.

01:40.080 --> 01:42.630
Independent security review remains a next step.

01:43.280 --> 01:46.640
The September prototype predates this competition and is disclosed.

01:46.640 --> 01:56.720
October work adds Ethereum mainnet metadata integration, preserves known drainer evidence during R P C outages, and adds a sample walkthrough using the shipped decoder and rules.

01:56.720 --> 01:58.950
Sample metadata is labelled as simulated.

01:59.600 --> 02:03.650
The consumer extension and decision rules stay free and open source.

02:03.650 --> 02:10.260
The proposed business is an integration package for wallet and application teams, with maintained rules and policy controls.

02:10.260 --> 02:12.460
Demand and pricing still need validation.

02:12.460 --> 02:14.670
No revenue or customers are claimed.

02:15.320 --> 02:18.810
Initial distribution would focus on Ethereum communities and educators.

02:18.810 --> 02:26.570
The next experiment is to observe five users, measure whether they understand requests, and record false alarms and completion time.

02:26.570 --> 02:31.220
Seeking two integration design partners is a future target, not existing traction.

02:31.870 --> 02:37.650
PlainSign's solo participant is Mohamed Adib, a student at Sri Venkateswara College of Engineering.

02:37.650 --> 02:43.420
The next milestones are independent security review, observed user testing and an integration pilot.

02:43.420 --> 02:47.950
The open repository and separate product demonstration make the implementation inspectable.
